<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Gandhara</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 518 BCE (approx.) · capital Taxila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An ancient kingdom of Taxila and the Peshawar valley, gateway between India and Iran, before it became a province of the Achaemenid Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gandhara, praised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> for its sheep's wool, became one of the sixteen mahajanapadas by the 7th and 6th centuries BCE. It covered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> valley and the land across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-emirate-of-afghanistan-2021-present">Afghanistan</a>, and controlled the routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Bactria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Buddhist texts record its king Pukkusati, said to have exchanged envoys and gifts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara of Magadha</a> and later to have become a monk. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> was already a centre of learning, where teachers taught the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, medicine and statecraft to students from across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Excavations at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhir-mound">Bhir Mound</a> reveal the early city with its irregular streets.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 518 BCE Gandhara appears as a subject land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun inscription</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus</a> or Darius, and it paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. Under Persian rule the Kharoshthi script developed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>. Local rule resurfaced later in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-taxila">Kingdom of Taxila</a> that Alexander met.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 700 BCE – 518 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:45.77%;width:3.62%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 518 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taxila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pukkusati</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gandhari Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gandhara, Gandhara Mahajanapada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-609&amp;focus=kingdom-of-gandhara" aria-label="Kingdom of Gandhara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6538 954 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Gandhara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6858 1210L6140 1251"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6858 1210h0"/><path class="map-dot-m